WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:11.830
As often happens when great men or great women
die, there's this effort to sort of boil down

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:15.820
their entire life to a single sentence at
the beginning of an obituary.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:22.180
You know, Lincoln gets two sentences: "He
freed the slaves," and "He saved the union."

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.810
Reagan gets: "He won the Cold War."

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:29.080
And people are looking to do the same thing
with Justice Scalia, and the problem is, that's

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.109
not how he set about to do things.

00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:37.510
He, first of all, has a huge legacy in specific
cases, but I think what he was really trying

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:44.680
to do is play the long game, and he wrote
his descents with law students in mind, with

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.620
future generations in mind.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:52.860
He wanted to put handholds on the cliff face
for future scholars, future lawyers, and future

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:58.930
justices to grab hold onto, to restore the
constitution to its proper role in American

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.710
life, and that is a multi-generational task.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.820
And that is who he was writing to, that's
who his audience was, and I think that is

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:06.650
his greatest legacy.

